# core/neighbours-reified - What relates to this element, and by which predicate?
#
# Answers: every relationship touching the focus element, in both directions, with
# both the qualified class (am:Serving) and the unqualified predicate the triple
# term names (am:serves).
#
# Does NOT prove: runtime dependency, traffic or criticality. Same caveat as every
# traversal template - a modelled relationship is a design-time statement.
#
# Prefer core/neighbours-qualified unless you specifically need the predicate.
# That one answers the same question about the same relationships, works on any
# SPARQL 1.1 engine, and does not depend on a bridge the converters do not yet
# emit. This template is for datasets whose author added the RDF 1.2 bridge and
# who need the predicate to report or to compare against an ontology.
#
# One hop only, deliberately. Chaining reified edges means an explicit self-join
# per hop: `<<( ?a ?p1 ?b )>>` then `<<( ?b ?p2 ?c )>>`. A property path cannot
# do it, because paths do not traverse triple terms. Two hops with qualified
# relationships is core/dependents-qualified.
#
# Requires SPARQL 1.2; refused unless the profile declares capabilities.rdf_reifies.
#
# Parameters: FOCUS_IRI, LIMIT
{{PREFIXES}}
SELECT ?direction ?predicate ?relType ?other ?otherLabel
WHERE {
  {{GRAPH_OPEN:semantic}}
    VALUES ?focus { {{FOCUS_IRI}} }
    ?rel a {{ROLE:relationship_class}} .
    {
      ?rel {{ROLE:reifies}} <<( ?focus ?predicate ?other )>> .
      BIND("outgoing" AS ?direction)
    }
    UNION
    {
      ?rel {{ROLE:reifies}} <<( ?other ?predicate ?focus )>> .
      BIND("incoming" AS ?direction)
    }
    OPTIONAL {
      ?rel a ?relType .
      FILTER(?relType != {{ROLE:relationship_class}} && ?relType != {{ROLE:concept_class}})
    }
    OPTIONAL { ?other {{PATH:label}} ?otherLabel }
  {{GRAPH_CLOSE}}
}
ORDER BY ?direction ?predicate ?other
LIMIT {{LIMIT}}
